answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bbdfe3e538e840385c082f16ca2cdfd07a7731c561d05cab6e7a25f487cdf463.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390524} {:answer/id #uuid "be9702e6-d21c-5c09-a96e-1267b5361dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd11656e3dae8a4976b1051a135b4bead1e5afc4beb21fa7541571892a037500.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390525} {:answer/id #uuid "35b1a419-9084-5fab-a034-b69305bdd882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/6674add26b7a15f5b09c53a832d649979484f0ffd41b440069dd5230cd9a5187.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390526} {:answer/id #uuid "724df295-2359-5b8b-ab62-27d9d0e10f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a30938f3d3af2fd70a0ee9498d7d99973d5386f062f22aaa8a028e9e18b3fb57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9702e6-d21c-5c09-a96e-1267b5361dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd11656e3dae8a4976b1051a135b4bead1e5afc4beb21fa7541571892a037500.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390525} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cb7b8b6-ddf5-57db-b6c9-dfd64245bb49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5100b042-b82c-5834-8756-fafba09daf0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-26815" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={6}^{x}-0.75?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={6}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/29/29d270676a48f4cf40625ef3ec50b0323c9c99389b2a7d080881727eda28de56.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={6}^{x}-0.75,$ we take the graph of $y={6}^{x}$ and shift it $0.75$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0.25),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-0.75.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b141be99e5c01c70d6f6f04f4adf06741f1dc0fcef80c73b52e3dba1ae44a87.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c898bbf-004c-5905-a0a0-761adefc8bd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Value of a Trigonometric Expression Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "078b25eb-c753-58c4-a8bb-c23711fdad11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290671} {:answer/id #uuid "7908b470-d7ec-55b9-b4ab-b86372edd49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291145} {:answer/id #uuid "820532c3-42ff-5bba-a8ac-2af534fa5f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291181} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdbc26c-f0b1-58b7-b55e-4663ee54eccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291327} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc2c5b1-4056-55a7-8817-839d1d89caec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "820532c3-42ff-5bba-a8ac-2af534fa5f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291181} :answer-field/id #uuid "d670500b-2a4c-531d-a949-3190b98d3ea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83f3d502-62d9-5e1b-a7be-52d9144a4c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-26907" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}))}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡({105}^{∘})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}⁡({105}^{∘}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}⁡({105}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}⁡({105}^{∘})]+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={105}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {105}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({210}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘})=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}))}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b053c8-c730-5718-875c-ec537ffaf2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290672} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff3acc2-6c5e-545c-9f4a-37f311a98b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290824} {:answer/id #uuid "3e98d6ea-3a9d-56e1-a895-1296def17348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290892} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8a892c-78b0-5ff5-9f7b-150ffa33b21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291439} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d58bd3-e35d-5e95-b414-0514297ac5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e98d6ea-3a9d-56e1-a895-1296def17348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290892} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9db0494-162a-5372-bb6c-4857b8b82143" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a646739-d7af-56d6-8602-58c253309f58" :question/math-academy-id "q-2698" :question/problem "Find the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-9x+3$ on the closed interval $-3\\le x\\le -1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-9x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}-12x-9,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}-12x-9 & =0 \\\\ -3({x}^{2}+4x+3) & =0 \\\\ -3(x+3)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-3,-1,$ which coincide with the endpoints of the given interval.\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-(-3)^{3}-6(-3)^{2}-9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}-9(-1)+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global minimum is $3.$\n\n![](images/2e/2eca6b83a3f99ca87a70e381490e99c98e80a535cd3051a4cdd0740f82de577b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043c68c8-e391-5780-a3ff-6d80dc52b081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290656} {:answer/id #uuid "931ab8d4-be2c-5e4d-92d6-fc6433eec3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291265} {:answer/id #uuid "9767e111-d1e9-5924-9dc2-6f58d60a1d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291290} {:answer/id #uuid "bb01b60e-6bb3-5d81-89d4-9256edcd3ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291443} {:answer/id #uuid "d869766d-8d2f-5348-b322-4dd0c0dc459d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043c68c8-e391-5780-a3ff-6d80dc52b081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290656} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15b4588-e9b5-5b8e-af3b-02394e3239c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69137884-05b3-53be-9c7c-95b53b1ed329" :question/math-academy-id "q-27009" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ \n\n- The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n- The function has a minimum value at  $x=2\\pi .$\n- The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c77bec4ebb04668fbff5176418ed9c09143a8660879fdf523a158c5f109006c.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ the function intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a maximum at $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1377c96f-21fc-51fb-8305-3bcbd43e0831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290712} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0db884-e2e8-5bb9-91dd-6d41ae5f204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290741} {:answer/id #uuid "50ebd29b-208a-5d56-90e2-e4954122c36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290978} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffa1fa3-01fa-5458-83f4-d55488f88570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291250} {:answer/id #uuid "c89344f3-53f2-5536-af33-917ad134ae7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ffa1fa3-01fa-5458-83f4-d55488f88570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291250} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1f01e4-776e-5a83-8671-70533062147c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1a15b56-fccd-50eb-8563-117b4ea3220a" :question/math-academy-id "q-270103" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{-12-x(x+12)}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12-x(x+12) & =-12-({x}^{2}+12x) \\\\  & =-12-({x}^{2}+12x+36-36) \\\\  & =-12-((x+6)^{2}-36) \\\\  & =24-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{-12-x(x+12)}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{24-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{24(1-(x+6)^{2}/24)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{24}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}/(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x+6)/2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x+6}{2\\sqrt{6}}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+6}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-6-\\sqrt{6}$ | $-6+\\sqrt{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{-12-x(x+12)}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{15}{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x+6)/2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}u. \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =15(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =15(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6edce2b1-4a27-55cc-b790-5b7035f56f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291108} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6aca7e-2c26-5ac1-ad8f-f26fea188d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291244} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fcb44c-262e-57ad-8f5a-95eb76595b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291334} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e240d4-f67f-53a1-917a-1652485d6081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291462} {:answer/id #uuid "da78814f-36ea-5458-8651-4b37fc6c9f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-5,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0e240d4-f67f-53a1-917a-1652485d6081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291462} :answer-field/id #uuid "204f716b-5c4a-54ac-ac8d-6cc04dca8468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7824abae-ac0a-561f-a7e4-242996de64c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-27068" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-3x-10>0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x-10 & >0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+2)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-2$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-2,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+2)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+2)(x-5)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+2)(x-5)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-3x-10>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31b90582-7492-541f-a422-a2ab4f6931f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290833} {:answer/id #uuid "4d187d1c-3040-5dd4-8232-8928c0010654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290959} {:answer/id #uuid "62cd106e-503e-5f0f-a812-7e330a9b4ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [3,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291054} {:answer/id #uuid "b69c52f4-8b11-5492-9898-5b48aeef799f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-4,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291421} {:answer/id #uuid "ce74bdfc-ed7d-5aed-ac88-c87e81a912a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31b90582-7492-541f-a422-a2ab4f6931f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290833} :answer-field/id #uuid "1252b4f0-0f19-5e91-821d-a4efb9cf7cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d81bc42-8840-5c1f-ae63-83e13f8b6083" :question/math-academy-id "q-27071" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $3{x}^{2}-3x-36\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3x-36 & \\le 0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-x-12) & \\le 0 \\\\ 3(x+3)(x-4) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+3)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-4)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $3(x+3)(x-4)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $3(x+3)(x-4)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,4).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(x+3)(x-4)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-3,4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b599f1e-71ef-5dac-88a7-d7a6b8c88246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290686} {:answer/id #uuid "5983021d-f7e6-5a87-8334-04f37b819ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291013} {:answer/id #uuid "5ece811e-cbe1-5481-a31c-64331b0fbbd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291037} {:answer/id #uuid "b9450f33-d113-5ab4-ada4-d129af5dc260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291435} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fc084f-3385-55c7-b348-c2cb4cc3ba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b599f1e-71ef-5dac-88a7-d7a6b8c88246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290686} :answer-field/id #uuid "51429ea0-f26a-52cb-9d46-1c28f1950ae2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28268741-19ff-50b9-9cfe-fa406e7a681a" :question/math-academy-id "q-27073" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $5{x}^{2}+25x-53\\ge 10x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+25x-53 & \\ge 10x-3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+15x-50 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+3x-10) & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5(x+5)(x-2) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $5(x+5)(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $5(x+5)(x-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $5(x+5)(x-2)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $5(x+5)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-5$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $5{x}^{2}+25x-53\\ge 10x-3$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5145dde8-60d2-54b7-ad8d-2892ff5d3963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4161037f51b8c4d95037c3ff28d7e6afecb354d4e0fabc22e5dd819bb65b6cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390533} {:answer/id #uuid "6680ac81-034b-5243-a46f-e14b15ca391f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4511ffac1899bdeb512b0b50b50b6a121477f486ef7e2efc83697fd9dddb8d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390534} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7b451c-b477-571c-b8f0-b6b471ddf99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/953bb642696a6b9c934627f978d150d27adbc7f73779a3d017c2ba714ff0835e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390535} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd2d67a-323e-5832-a842-a36b0a6fce73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.